"""
Latitude events in Julian Days (Sun..Pluto) -- RIGOROUS edition
=================================================================
Events (geocentric ecliptic latitude)
  MAX_LATITUDE   local maximum: the body turns back towards the south
  MIN_LATITUDE   local minimum: the body turns back towards the north
  LATITUDE_ZERO  latitude crosses 0, i.e. the body crosses the ecliptic
                 S->N = ascending node, N->S = descending node

Same flag-controlled design as the longitude script: swisseph only samples,
roots come from NumPy Chebyshev polynomials, every event is re-solved against
the real ephemeris, and the result is proved complete before it is printed.

LAYERS OF RIGOR
 1. FIT VALIDATION   - window accepted only if tail coefficients are small AND the
                       polynomial matches swisseph at 7 held-out points.
 2. MONOTONIC PIECES - each window is cut at every verified MAX/MIN; inside a piece
                       "does latitude cross 0?" is decided by the TRUE latitude at
                       the two ends, so a grazing MAX/MIN near 0 cannot hide a
                       crossing.
 3. SEAM OVERLAP     - windows evaluated on [-1-EXT, 1+EXT]; duplicates merged.
 4. POLISH           - MAX/MIN re-solved on the true latitude speed, crossings on
                       the true latitude (always inside a true bracket).
 5. CONTINUITY CHECK - one state machine (side of the ecliptic, direction of
                       motion) replays every event: MAX/MIN alternate, crossings
                       alternate, a crossing must go the way the body is moving,
                       and every MAX/MIN must lie on the side the crossings say.
 6. INDEPENDENT AUDIT- true latitude and true latitude speed read from swisseph
                       every AUDIT_STEP days; replaying the events between two
                       samples must land on the true state.

All events are always computed (the check needs every one of them); the SHOW_*
flags only filter the table.

NOTES
 * Latitude is the same in the tropical and the (default) sidereal zodiac, so
   there is no SIDEREAL switch. ECLIPTIC_J2000 = True measures from the fixed
   J2000 ecliptic instead of the ecliptic of date.
 * Rahu/Ketu are not offered: the lunar nodes lie on the ecliptic (latitude 0).
 * The Sun's geocentric latitude is not exactly 0: the Earth swings around the
   Earth-Moon barycentre, which gives under 1 arcsecond with a period of about a
   month. Its events are real but tiny.
 * TOPOCENTRIC = True adds the Moon's parallax (up to ~1 deg); the Moon's
   topocentric latitude then has a MAX/MIN almost every day.
"""
import time
import bisect
import csv
import numpy as np
import swisseph as swe
from numpy.polynomial import chebyshev as C
from concurrent.futures import ProcessPoolExecutor

# =====================================================================
#                              CONFIG
# =====================================================================
# ---- time range (Julian Day, UT; no datetime used) --------------------
START_DATE = (2026, 1, 1, 0.0)    # (year, month, day, hour) -> converted with swe.julday
START_JD   = None                  # set a JD here to override START_DATE
DAYS       = 365                   # length of range
PAD        = 3.0                   # compute this many days outside the range (edge safety)
START_DATE_IS_LOCAL = True         # True -> START_DATE is IST (TZ_OFFSET_HOURS), not UT

# ---- time display -----------------------------------------------------
SHOW_JD    = False                 # JD (UT) column
SHOW_IST   = True                  # local time column: YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS
TZ_OFFSET_HOURS = 5.5              # IST = UT + 5:30 (change for another zone)
TZ_LABEL   = "IST"                 # column heading

